BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:camargo-fellowship-2027-2028@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261031T115744Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261001
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261002
SUMMARY:Deadline: Camargo Fellowship 2027-2028 for Artists\, Scholars and T
 hinkers (Cassis\, France)
DESCRIPTION:The Camargo Foundation invites artists\, scholars and thinkers 
 to its flagship Core Fellowship: a ten-week self-directed residency on its
  clifftop campus overlooking the Mediterranean in Cassis\, France\, dedica
 ted to research\, experimentation and interdisciplinary exchange. Curators
  apply under the Thinkers category\, which covers accomplished professiona
 ls and practitioners in cultural and creative fields who are professionall
 y engaged in critical thought\, including curators\, critics and journalis
 ts\; projects should engage theoretical\, artistic or societal questions. 
 WHAT YOU GET: a stipend of EUR 350 per week (EUR 3\,500 total)\, basic tra
 nsportation funding\, and a furnished apartment with an equipped kitchen o
 n campus. Fourteen fellowships are awarded per year: seven for artists and
  seven for scholars and thinkers. RESIDENCY PERIODS: Fall 2027 (8 Septembe
 r to 17 November 2027) or Spring 2028 (23 February to 4 May 2028). ELIGIBI
 LITY: artists in visual arts\, performance\, writing\, film\, music or mul
 tidisciplinary work with at least 5 years of professional practice\; schol
 ars holding or pursuing a PhD focused on French and Francophone cultures o
 r cross-cultural Mediterranean studies\; and thinkers as described above. 
 APPLICATION: via Submittable with a 100-word proposal summary\, a 1\,200-w
 ord proposal narrative\, a 300-word rationale for Camargo\, a 300-word car
 eer-stage rationale\, a CV (max 3 pages)\, work samples and two references
 . NOTE: the foundation has announced that this is the final Fellowship sel
 ected through a public open call until further notice\; a new selection pr
 ocess begins in July 2027\, making this the last chance to apply directly.
 \n\nOrganisation: Camargo Foundation\nLocation: Camargo Foundation campus\
 , Cassis\, France. Open worldwide.\nAward: EUR 350 per week (EUR 3\,500 to
 tal for the ten-week residency)\, plus basic travel funding and a furnishe
 d apartment with equipped kitchen on the Camargo campus. 14 fellowships pe
 r year: 7 for artists and 7 for scholars and thinkers. No application fee.
 \nDuration: Ten weeks\, either 8 September to 17 November 2027 or 23 Febru
 ary to 4 May 2028.\n\nMore: https://camargofoundation.org/en/camargo-fello
 wship-2026-2027?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://camargofoundation.org/en/camargo-fellowship-2026-2027?utm_sourc
 e=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Camargo Foundation campus\, Cassis\, France. Open worldwide.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:tech-policy-press-fellowship-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261031T115744Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260924
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260925
SUMMARY:Deadline: Tech Policy Press Fellowship Program 2027
DESCRIPTION:Tech Policy Press is recruiting its 2027 fellowship cohort to p
 ursue independent reporting and analysis on urgent questions at the inters
 ection of technology and democracy. Since 2023 the programme has supported
  25 fellows from 11 countries across three cohorts\, writing on AI governa
 nce\, tech regulation\, digital rights and the entanglement of state and c
 orporate power. WHO IT IS FOR: mid-career journalists\, researchers\, lawy
 ers and public policy professionals with a strong background in a specific
  tech policy area\, a particular geography\, or technology's impact on dem
 ocracy and society. Applicants from the Global Majority and from communiti
 es underrepresented in media are encouraged\, as are projects that centre 
 communities and regions usually excluded from global tech policy conversat
 ions. Contributions can range from original reporting to policy analysis\,
  and technical practitioners are welcome as long as the work fits the publ
 ication's editorial purview. EXPECTATIONS: at least one piece of analysis 
 or reporting per month (exclusive to Tech Policy Press\, though it may be 
 republished elsewhere)\, a mandatory hour-long monthly cohort meeting\, a 
 monthly check-in with the editorial team\, collaboration with other fellow
 s and the masthead\, agreed deadlines\, and disclosure of conflicts of int
 erest. Applicants should arrive with a clear area of focus and a sense of 
 how the work will inform both the public and decision-makers. APPLY via th
 e online form by 11:59pm EDT on 24 September 2026\, with a CV or resume\, 
 a cover letter of no more than two pages\, links to prior publications (op
 en access\, published within the past three years\, at least one in Englis
 h and at least one solo-authored\; social posts\, pre-prints and unpublish
 ed coursework do not count) and two professional references. Shortlisted c
 andidates may be invited to an online interview. Decisions are expected by
  early December 2026. Two optional Zoom information sessions run on 9 Sept
 ember at 8am EDT and 16 September at 7pm EDT\; registration required.\n\nO
 rganisation: Tech Policy Press\nLocation: Fully remote\, worldwide. Intern
 ational applicants explicitly encouraged\; fellows work as independent con
 tractors. No requirement to be authorised to work in the USA.\nAward: USD 
 10\,000 stipend per fellow\, paid quarterly. Also included: editorial guid
 ance from the Tech Policy Press team\, membership of an international coho
 rt with regular feedback and exchange\, and monthly sessions to strengthen
  the work. Fellows' work is published on the Tech Policy Press website and
  promoted through the newsletter\, podcast and events. No application fee.
 \nDuration: Twelve months\, part-time\, running January to December 2027\n
 \nMore: https://www.techpolicy.press/call-for-applications-2027-tech-polic
 y-press-fellowship-program/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medi
 um=referral
URL:https://www.techpolicy.press/call-for-applications-2027-tech-policy-pre
 ss-fellowship-program/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=re
 ferral
LOCATION:Fully remote\, worldwide. International applicants explicitly enco
 uraged\; fellows work as independent contractors. No requirement to be aut
 horised to work in the USA.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:mcgraw-business-journalism-fellowship-fall-2026@artificialnouveau.githu
 b.io
DTSTAMP:20261031T115744Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261012
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261013
SUMMARY:Deadline: McGraw Fellowship for Business Journalism - Fall 2026 cyc
 le
DESCRIPTION:Fellowships supporting enterprise and investigative reporting w
 ith a business or economic angle\, from the Harold W. McGraw\, Jr. Center 
 for Business Journalism at the Newmark Graduate School of Journalism\, CUN
 Y. Tech-platform economies\, AI labour\, data-broker investigations\, digi
 tal-economy beats and similar tech-and-business stories fit naturally. ELI
 GIBILITY: working journalists with at least 5 years of professional experi
 ence\; freelancers and staff are both eligible worldwide. CYCLES: Fall 202
 6 cycle deadline 12 October 2026\; spring cycle expected around April 2027
 . No application fee.\n\nOrganisation: Harold W. McGraw\, Jr. Center for B
 usiness Journalism (Newmark Graduate School of Journalism\, CUNY)\nLocatio
 n: Worldwide (freelancers and staff)\nAward: Up to USD 15\,000 per fellows
 hip\, plus editorial supervision and placement assistance. No application 
 fee.\nDuration: Project-based\; fellowship typically runs alongside the re
 ported project\n\nMore: https://www.mcgrawcenter.org/the-harold-w-mcgraw-j
 r-business-journalism-fellowships/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&u
 tm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.mcgrawcenter.org/the-harold-w-mcgraw-jr-business-journalism
 -fellowships/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Worldwide (freelancers and staff)
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:crp-research-fellowship-programme-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261031T115744Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260910
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260911
SUMMARY:Deadline: CRP Research Fellowship Programme 2026
DESCRIPTION:Short-term international research fellowship for scientists see
 king placements at host institutions in participating member countries. Fe
 llowships last 6 to 26 weeks and support international scientific collabor
 ation\, research mobility\, knowledge exchange and cross-border partnershi
 ps. PRIORITY THEMES: sustainable agricultural productivity\, climate actio
 n\, environmental protection\, biodiversity conservation\, soil health\, w
 ater resource sustainability\, knowledge innovation\, sustainable livestoc
 k systems\, fisheries development and aquaculture sustainability. FUNDING:
  a travel lump sum\, return economy airfare\, a weekly subsistence allowan
 ce (EUR 600 or EUR 650 by host-country cost of living) and a EUR 165 termi
 nal allowance\; insurance\, visa fees\, lab/bench fees and family or perso
 nal costs are not covered. ELIGIBILITY: employed by or affiliated with an 
 institution in a participating country\; proposed host institution in a di
 fferent participating country\; at least 4 years of postdoctoral experienc
 e (exceptional candidates with equivalent expertise and a strong publicati
 on record may also be considered). Applicants must not already hold a posi
 tion at the host institution\, need employer approval and assurance of con
 tinued employment or affiliation after the fellowship\, and previous fello
 ws may reapply only after a 5-year gap. Applicants confirm country eligibi
 lity\, secure a host institution and collaboration\, prepare a research pr
 oposal (objective\, scientific relevance\, methodology\, expected outcomes
 \, 6-26 week timeline\, collaboration benefits) and submit supporting docu
 ments (CV\, publication list\, employer approval\, host acceptance letter\
 , proof of affiliation).\n\nOrganisation: CRP Research Fellowship Programm
 e\nLocation: Participating member countries (host institution must be in a
  different participating country from the applicant's home institution)\nA
 ward: Travel lump-sum allowance plus return economy airfare\, a weekly sub
 sistence allowance of EUR 600 or EUR 650 (depending on host-country cost o
 f living)\, and a fixed EUR 165 terminal allowance. Not covered: insurance
 \, visa fees\, lab/bench fees\, family travel\, and personal or dependents
 ' costs.\nDuration: 6 to 26 weeks (short-term research placement abroad)\n
 \nMore: https://www2.fundsforngos.org/innovation/call-for-applications-crp
 -research-fellowship-programme/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_
 medium=referral
URL:https://www2.fundsforngos.org/innovation/call-for-applications-crp-rese
 arch-fellowship-programme/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_mediu
 m=referral
LOCATION:Participating member countries (host institution must be in a diff
 erent participating country from the applicant's home institution)
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:wilson-center-research-fellowship-2027-2028@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261031T115744Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260930
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261001
SUMMARY:Deadline: Wilson Center Research Fellowship\, 2027-2028 Class
DESCRIPTION:The Wilson Center\, the congressionally chartered nonpartisan p
 olicy research institution in Washington DC\, awards 10 to 20 research fel
 lowships for its 2027-2028 class. Fellows conduct research and write in th
 eir areas of interest while interacting with policymakers in Washington an
 d with other fellows. The Center accepts policy-relevant\, nonpartisan pro
 posals that address key foreign policy challenges confronting the United S
 tates and the world\, and projects must align with at least one of the Cen
 ter's four scholarship pillars: strategic competition\, economic statecraf
 t\, TECHNOLOGY AND INNOVATION\, and regional scholarship. The technology a
 nd innovation pillar is the relevant route for AI governance\, emerging te
 chnology and digital policy projects\, which the Center also supports thro
 ugh its Science and Technology Innovation Program. Fellows work remotely f
 or the duration of the fellowship\; the Center covers travel costs for two
  required trips to Washington DC at the beginning and the conclusion of th
 e term. The stipend is USD 10\,000 per month for the twelve-month term\, w
 hich runs 1 June 2027 to 30 May 2028. ELIGIBILITY: academic candidates nee
 d a PhD in hand and either a published book or monograph beyond the disser
 tation\, or a substantial body of research articles (five or more) in repu
 table peer-reviewed journals. Non-academic applicants (practitioners\, jou
 rnalists\, public intellectuals) need at least ten years of relevant exper
 ience and a record of substantial policy impact. There is no citizenship r
 estriction\, but English proficiency is required. Applications\, including
  letters of recommendation\, must be submitted online via Interfolio by 11
 :59pm ET on 30 September 2026. Questions: fellowships@wilsoncenter.org. NO
 TE: this is an open-topic foreign policy fellowship\, not an AI-specific o
 ne\; the technology angle depends on how you scope the proposal.\n\nOrgani
 sation: Woodrow Wilson International Center for Scholars\nLocation: Remote
  for the duration of the fellowship\, with two required trips to Washingto
 n DC (at the start and the end) for which travel costs are covered.\nAward
 : USD 10\,000 per month for a twelve-month fellowship\, plus covered trave
 l for the two required trips to Washington DC. No application fee.\nDurati
 on: Twelve months\, 1 June 2027 to 30 May 2028\n\nMore: https://www.wilson
 center.org/research-fellowship?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_m
 edium=referral
URL:https://www.wilsoncenter.org/research-fellowship?utm_source=artificialn
 ouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Remote for the duration of the fellowship\, with two required trip
 s to Washington DC (at the start and the end) for which travel costs are c
 overed.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:open-notebook-early-career-fellowship-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261031T115744Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261031
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261101
SUMMARY:Deadline: The Open Notebook Early-Career Fellowship 2027 (remote)
DESCRIPTION:The Open Notebook's paid fellowship for early-career science jo
 urnalists is open for its 2027 round. Fellows spend twelve months working 
 part-time and remotely with a personal mentor\, reporting and writing arti
 cles about the craft of science journalism that are published at The Open 
 Notebook\, one of the field's central craft resources. Eligibility: early-
 career science journalists with fewer than three years of regular professi
 onal science-writing experience\; applicants from anywhere in the world ar
 e welcome\, and application materials are in English. TO APPLY: submit the
  application form (linked from the program page) by 31 October 2026\, 11:5
 9 PM US Central time\; reference letters are due 7 November 2026. The fell
 owship year starts 9 February 2027.\n\nOrganisation: The Open Notebook\nLo
 cation: Remote\, worldwide. Application materials in English\; internation
 al applicants welcome.\nAward: USD 6\,600 stipend over the twelve-month pa
 rt-time fellowship\, plus one-on-one mentoring from an experienced science
  journalist and published clips at The Open Notebook. No application fee.\
 nDuration: 12 months part-time\, starting 9 February 2027.\n\nMore: https:
 //www.theopennotebook.com/early-career-fellowship-program/?utm_source=arti
 ficialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.theopennotebook.com/early-career-fellowship-program/?utm_so
 urce=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Remote\, worldwide. Application materials in English\; internation
 al applicants welcome.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:gias-gulbenkian-fellowships-2027-28@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261031T115744Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261015
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261016
SUMMARY:Deadline: Gulbenkian Institute for Advanced Study Fellowships 2027/
 28 (Lisbon)
DESCRIPTION:Residential think-tank fellowships at the Calouste Gulbenkian F
 oundation in Lisbon\, explicitly open to artists as well as academics (aff
 iliated or independent)\, emeritus professors and professionals outside ac
 ademia. Fellows spend 3 to 8 months pursuing self-directed work in any dom
 ain\, provided it needs no laboratory or experimental infrastructure and i
 s not predominantly applied\; selection weighs the CV at 40 percent and th
 e innovativeness of the proposal at 60 percent. WHAT YOU GET: travel\, tra
 vel and health insurance\, a monthly stipend and a partial housing subsidy
 \, with fellow status continuing after the stay. ELIGIBILITY: open interna
 tionally\; past GIAS fellows must wait five years. Applications via the My
 Gulbenkian platform with CV\, a short description of proposed activities a
 nd two referee names. DEADLINE: the annual window runs 1 July to 15 Octobe
 r\; this round closes 15 October 2026 for 2027/28 stays.\n\nOrganisation: 
 Gulbenkian Institute for Advanced Study (GIAS)\, Calouste Gulbenkian Found
 ation\nLocation: Lisbon\, Portugal. Open internationally\; residential fel
 lowships at the Gulbenkian in Lisbon.\nAward: Up to 15 fellowships a year.
  Each covers travel costs\, travel and health insurance\, a monthly stipen
 d and a partial housing subsidy\; the stipend level is set at selection ba
 sed on CV and length of stay. No application fee.\nDuration: 3 to 8 months
  during the 2027/28 academic year\n\nMore: https://gulbenkian.pt/gias/?utm
 _source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://gulbenkian.pt/gias/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_m
 edium=referral
LOCATION:Lisbon\, Portugal. Open internationally\; residential fellowships 
 at the Gulbenkian in Lisbon.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:ku-leuven-ai-safety-lab-fellowships-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261031T115744Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261031
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261101
SUMMARY:Deadline: KU Leuven AI Safety Lab: Visiting Fellowships 2027 (Leuve
 n)
DESCRIPTION:First visiting-fellow cohort of the newly launched KU Leuven AI
  Safety Lab\, a multidisciplinary research group on large-scale risks of a
 dvanced AI (independent research\, policy workshops\, a summer school and 
 an annual conference). Five fellows spend March to May 2027 in Leuven unde
 r two programmes: Theories of Victory and Viable Endgames (positive AI fut
 ures and the conditions for beneficial advanced AI outcomes) and Democrati
 c Control over AI Companies (power concentration and mechanisms for societ
 al oversight of frontier AI developers)\; strong AI safety proposals outsi
 de these two areas are also considered. ELIGIBILITY: PhD candidates\, post
 doctoral researchers\, faculty and researchers outside academia\, from any
  relevant discipline\; applications from underrepresented groups encourage
 d. APPLICATION: email a CV (max 2 pages including publications)\, a one-pa
 ge cover letter and a research plan (max 1\,500 words) to existentialrisk@
 kuleuven.be with subject line 'Fellowship application' plus the programme 
 name. Deadline 31 October 2026.\n\nOrganisation: KU Leuven AI Safety Lab\n
 Location: Leuven\, Belgium. Open internationally\; three months in residen
 ce.\nAward: EUR 2\,000 per month for three months\, covering accommodation
  and living costs\, plus office space\, library access and KU Leuven facil
 ities. Five fellowships. No application fee.\nDuration: Three months\, Mar
 ch to May 2027\n\nMore: https://www.aisafetylab.eu/open-calls/?utm_source=
 artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.aisafetylab.eu/open-calls/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugran
 tdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Leuven\, Belgium. Open internationally\; three months in residence
 .
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:internet-society-fellowship-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261031T115744Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261218
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261219
SUMMARY:Deadline: Internet Society Fellowship Program 2027
DESCRIPTION:The Internet Society Fellowship supports people advancing Inter
 net connectivity\, policy or security in their communities. Fellows design
  and implement a practical project with deliverables such as policy briefs
 \, research reports\, training programs\, community engagement initiatives
 \, workshops or technical resources\, supported by a stipend of up to USD 
 5\,000\, a dedicated mentor\, weekly virtual training sessions and funded 
 travel to a global or regional Internet event. IMPORTANT PREREQUISITE: app
 licants must first complete the Pre-Fellowship Training modules\; enrollme
 nt for the training runs 23 September to 22 November 2026\, and eligible p
 articipants receive the Fellowship application link by email within 24 hou
 rs of completing it. ELIGIBILITY: open worldwide\; requires a high level o
 f English reading\, speaking and writing\, continuous Internet access thro
 ughout the program\, a valid passport (six months beyond anticipated trave
 l)\, at least four hours per week over 10 months\, and not currently parti
 cipating in a 2026 Internet Society fellowship. TIMELINE: applications rev
 iewed on a rolling basis and close 18 December 2026\; notifications 19 Feb
 ruary 2027\; program kick-off 22 February 2027\; project execution 1 June 
 to 30 November 2027\; graduation early December 2027.\n\nOrganisation: Int
 ernet Society\nLocation: Remote\, worldwide\, with funded travel to one gl
 obal or regional Internet event. Requires continuous Internet access\, str
 ong English\, and a passport valid at least six months beyond the anticipa
 ted travel.\nAward: Project stipend of up to USD 5\,000 (upon submission a
 nd approval of the project budget)\, plus travel support to attend a globa
 l or regional Internet event and a dedicated mentor aligned with the proje
 ct area. No application fee.\nDuration: 10 months (kick-off 22 February 20
 27\, graduation early December 2027)\, including a six-month mentored proj
 ect execution period from 1 June to 30 November 2027. Minimum commitment o
 f four hours per week.\n\nMore: https://www.internetsociety.org/fellowship
 /?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://www.internetsociety.org/fellowship/?utm_source=artificialnouvea
 ugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Remote\, worldwide\, with funded travel to one global or regional 
 Internet event. Requires continuous Internet access\, strong English\, and
  a passport valid at least six months beyond the anticipated travel.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:mensch-maschine-visit-fellowship-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261031T115744Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261201
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261202
SUMMARY:Deadline: Mensch Maschine VISIT Residency Fellowship 2027: Energy G
 hosts (E-WERK Luckenwalde / Akademie der Künste)
DESCRIPTION:The Mensch Maschine VISIT Residency Program is an annual fellow
 ship offering four fully funded fellowships to artists from around the wor
 ld\, run by the JUNGE AKADEMIE of the Akademie der Künste (Berlin) with V
 ISIT\, the artist-in-residence programme of the E.ON Foundation\, E-WERK L
 uckenwalde and Ujazdowski Castle Centre for Contemporary Art (Warsaw). The
  2027 edition particularly encourages projects at the intersection of art\
 , energy and ecology exploring the theme ENERGY GHOSTS: the unseen dimensi
 ons of energy approached through mythology\, cosmology or speculative fict
 ion\, the invisible infrastructures\, histories and residues that haunt co
 ntemporary life\, and energy as an ecological\, political or spiritual for
 ce. Proposals are welcome from visual art\, performance\, film\, installat
 ion\, sound\, writing and new media. WHAT YOU GET: EUR 20\,000 per fellow 
 for artist fees and production costs of a new project (research and develo
 pment\, travel\, material and presentation costs)\; three fellows receive 
 studio space and accommodation at E-WERK Luckenwalde (a former power stati
 on about 40 minutes south of Berlin)\, one is accommodated by the Akademie
  der Künste in Berlin. The three-month residency runs roughly September t
 o November 2027 with on-site presence of at least 75% required\, a retreat
  at Ujazdowski Castle in Warsaw in September 2027\, events across the thre
 e venues\, and a closing symposium in October 2027 at the Akademie der Kü
 nste where fellows show work in progress alongside the 2026 cohort. JURY: 
 Nolan Oswald Dennis\; Chus Martinez (Institute Art Gender Nature\, TBA21)\
 ; Sarah Johanna Theurer (Fondation Beyeler)\; Anh-Linh Ngo (Akademie der K
 ünste)\; and Tiara Roxanne. APPLICATION: via the VISIT portal at visit-en
 ergy.com/bewerben. DEADLINE: 1 December 2026\, 23:59 CET.\n\nOrganisation:
  JUNGE AKADEMIE of the Akademie der Künste\, VISIT (E.ON Foundation)\, E-
 WERK Luckenwalde and Ujazdowski Castle Centre for Contemporary Art\nLocati
 on: E-WERK Luckenwalde\, Brandenburg\, Germany (3 fellows) and Akademie de
 r Künste\, Berlin (1 fellow)\, with a retreat at Ujazdowski Castle CCA in
  Warsaw\, Poland. Open to artists worldwide.\nAward: Four fully funded fel
 lowships of EUR 20\,000 each\, intended for artist fees and production cos
 ts of a new project (research and development\, travel\, materials and pre
 sentation costs)\, plus studio space and accommodation for the three-month
  residency.\nDuration: Three months\, roughly September to November 2027 (
 the AdK notice gives mid-August to mid-November)\; fellows must live and w
 ork on site at least 75% of the time.\n\nMore: https://adk.de/news/2026/10
 /open-call-mensch-maschine-stipendium?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdes
 k&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://adk.de/news/2026/10/open-call-mensch-maschine-stipendium?utm_so
 urce=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:E-WERK Luckenwalde\, Brandenburg\, Germany (3 fellows) and Akademi
 e der Künste\, Berlin (1 fellow)\, with a retreat at Ujazdowski Castle CC
 A in Warsaw\, Poland. Open to artists worldwide.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:jsk-journalism-fellowship-2027-28@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261031T115744Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261202
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261203
SUMMARY:Deadline: JSK Journalism Fellowship 2027-28 (John S. Knight Fellows
 hips at Stanford)
DESCRIPTION:Stanford's nine-month residential fellowship for journalists an
 d people who work in service of journalism (editors\, producers\, photogra
 phers\, audience and community builders\, publishers\, operations and stra
 tegy staff). Fellows bring a question about journalism they cannot let go 
 of rather than a fixed project\, and pursue it with a small international 
 cohort\, individual coaching\, customised workshops and the run of Stanfor
 d\; many fellows work on AI\, platforms\, information ecosystems and audie
 nce trust. ELIGIBILITY: at least five years of professional experience (st
 udent work and internships don't count)\; no degree or newsroom background
  required\; freelancers must do most of their work in journalism. Not elig
 ible: PR or communications staff\, government employees\, full-time academ
 ics\, staff of non-editorially-independent media\, degree seekers\, book o
 r reporting projects. APPLICATION: via SurveyMonkey Apply (jsk.smapply.org
 )\; deadline 2 December 2026 at 1pm Pacific\, no extensions. Info webinars
  on 21 October and 5\, 6 and 12 November 2026.\n\nOrganisation: John S. Kn
 ight Journalism Fellowships at Stanford\nLocation: Stanford\, California\,
  USA (residential 9-month fellowship). Citizens of countries where the US 
 has suspended J visa issuance cannot be offered a fellowship unless they a
 pply with another unrestricted citizenship.\nAward: USD 135\,000 stipend f
 or nine months\, plus Stanford tuition and health insurance and a health s
 upplement for dependents on Stanford coverage. No application fee.\nDurati
 on: 9 months in residence at Stanford\, September 2027 to May 2028\n\nMore
 : https://jsk.stanford.edu/become-a-fellow?utm_source=artificialnouveaugra
 ntdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://jsk.stanford.edu/become-a-fellow?utm_source=artificialnouveaugr
 antdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Stanford\, California\, USA (residential 9-month fellowship). Citi
 zens of countries where the US has suspended J visa issuance cannot be off
 ered a fellowship unless they apply with another unrestricted citizenship.
END:VEVENT
